      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the first term is $-1$ and the second term is $-3.$",
      "worked_solution": "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence (in function notation) is\n\n$$\nf(n)=f(1)+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nWe are given the first term $f(1)=-1.$ Also, the common difference of the sequence can be found as\n\n$$\nd=-3-(-1)=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-1+(n-1)(-2) \\\\  & =-1-2n+2 \\\\  & =1-2n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is $f(n)=1-2n.$",
